"Let ${\\mathcal C}$ be an $[n,k,d]_q$ generalized Reed–Solomon (GRS) code, and let $\\operatorname{GRS\\text{-}cover}$ denote the covering algorithm. The **average number of punctures** is the expected number of coordinates punctured before the algorithm succeeds in returning a codeword within the covering radius.\n\n**Puncture-count conjecture.** For an $[n,k,d]_q$ GRS code, the average number of punctures needed for $\\operatorname{GRS\\text{-}cover}$ to succeed in returning a codeword within the covering radius is less than\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\nc_1(d-1) & \\text{when } \\operatorname{GRS\\text{-}decode \\text{ is a unique decoder},\\\\\nc_2 & \\text{when } \\operatorname{GRS\\text{-}decode}=\\operatorname{GS} \\text{ is the list decoder},\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nfor some positive constants $c_1<1$ and $c_2$.\n\nThe source later states that the first part does not hold in general; the second part remains unresolved there. Thus this combined conjecture is refuted, with its list-decoder component still open."